# ============================================================================
# ToolSeer V3 — apps/web/_redirects   (Cloudflare Pages)
#
# RULES OF THIS FILE, verified against the Cloudflare Pages redirects docs:
#   * FIRST MATCH WINS, top to bottom — most specific rules are listed first.
#   * Static paths come before dynamic (placeholder) paths.
#   * Redirects run BEFORE static asset matching and are always followed, so no
#     rule may be broad enough to match /assets/*, /vendor/*, /sw.js,
#     /manifest.webmanifest, /offline.html, /robots.txt, /sitemap.xml or any real
#     tool slug. A rule that did would break the live site instead of helping a
#     legacy visitor. Every rule below is checked against those namespaces.
#   * Splats are used only in the documented trailing form (`/p/*`), which is also
#     the only form tools/serve.mjs implements. One splat per line, at the end.
#   * Fragments (#view-converter, #view-works, #view-contact) are NEVER evaluated
#     by Cloudflare. They need no rule: the browser keeps the fragment across a
#     redirect whose Location has none, so an old link
#     `/p/toolseer.html#view-works` becomes `/#view-works` and lands on the new
#     hub. See docs/CONTINUITY.md §3.
#   * Cloudflare cannot match query strings: a source containing "?" is not
#     usable. That is why `/?m=1` (the old Blogger mobile variant) has no rule
#     below — see §5. It already resolves to the responsive hub.
#   * Limits: 2,000 static + 100 dynamic rules. This file uses about 40.
#
# LOCAL PREVIEW WARNING: tools/serve.mjs understands exact paths and trailing
# `/*` only — it does NOT implement `:placeholder` sources. Rules marked
# [dynamic] below will silently not fire on http://127.0.0.1:4173. Test those
# against Cloudflare, or add placeholder support to tools/serve.mjs.
#
# ============================================================================
# ⚠ OWNER MUST VERIFY — we do NOT have the real Blogger URL list yet.
#
# Everything from section 3 down is a SHAPE inferred from how Blogger emits
# URLs, not from this site's actual traffic. Confirm against:
#   a) Blogger dashboard → Settings → the blog's published URL / post list, and
#   b) Google Search Console → Indexing → Pages (export the indexed URLs).
# Then keep every shape that matches, delete the ones that do not, and add any
# real URL that is missing. Section 6 lists likely aliases, commented out, to
# switch on ONLY if they appear in that list.
# ============================================================================


# --- 1. Canonical, no-trailing-slash normalisation (existing contract) -------
# These are the real routes (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3). Keep them first so no
# dynamic rule can ever shadow a live page.

/ / 200
/image-to-pdf/ /image-to-pdf 301
/merge-pdf/ /merge-pdf 301
/compress-pdf/ /compress-pdf 301
/split-pdf/ /split-pdf 301
/protect-pdf/ /protect-pdf 301
/unlock-pdf/ /unlock-pdf 301
/pdf-to-images/ /pdf-to-images 301
/organize-pdf/ /organize-pdf 301
/extract-text-pdf/ /extract-text-pdf 301
/desktop/ /desktop 301
/works/ /works 301
/contact/ /contact 301
/privacy/ /privacy 301
/terms/ /terms 301

# The physical file behind a route must canonicalise to the route. This line is
# also what protects every /<route>/index.html from the two-segment dynamic rules
# in section 4 (first match wins). [dynamic]
/:slug/index.html /:slug 301


# --- 2. Legacy single-page views and entry paths (VERIFY) --------------------
# The old site was one page; these were its obvious entry points.

/convert / 301
/converter / 301
/our-works /works 301
/contact-us /contact 301
/contact_us /contact 301
/index.html / 301
/home / 301
