# --- 3. Blogger static pages: /p/<slug>.html (VERIFY) ------------------------
# Blogger publishes static pages under /p/. Splat at the end, so it can never
# match a real route (nothing on this site lives under /p/).

/p/* / 301
/p / 301
/p/ / 301


# --- 4. Blogger dated posts and archives (VERIFY) ---------------------------
# Blogger's permalink shape is /YYYY/MM/slug.html, with /YYYY/MM/ as the monthly
# archive listing. Listed most specific first. [dynamic] — see the local preview
# warning above.
#
# TWO-SEGMENT RULES AND WHAT THEY CAN TOUCH. `/:year/:month` and `/:year/:month/`
# match any two-segment path. Everything this site actually serves at depth two is
# either /<route>/index.html — already redirected above by /:slug/index.html — or
# the single build artifact /vendor/vendor.lock.json, which no page ever fetches
# and which therefore redirects to the hub harmlessly. If the owner wants even that
# gone, delete the two `/:year/:month` lines and enumerate literal years instead
# (`/2019/* / 301`, `/2020/* / 301`, …), which cannot match anything else.
#
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT: `/:year/ / 301` (Blogger's yearly archive). A one-segment
# placeholder would match EVERY one-segment path on the site — /sw.js, /robots.txt,
# /sitemap.xml, /offline.html, /manifest.webmanifest and every tool route — if
# Cloudflare ever normalises trailing slashes during matching. That downside is
# catastrophic and the upside is one rare legacy URL shape, so the rule is not
# here. If the archive widget is set to yearly, add literal years only.

/:year/:month/:day/:slug.html / 301
/:year/:month/:slug.html / 301
/:year/:month/ / 301
/:year/:month / 301


# --- 5. Feeds, search, labels and the Blogger mobile variant (VERIFY) --------
# Feed paths have no equivalent on the new site, so they go to the hub rather
# than 404. Search and label URLs also land on the hub, which is the page that
# links every tool.
#
# /?m=1 CANNOT be expressed here: the source path is "/" and Cloudflare does not
# match query strings. It needs no rule — the query is inert on a responsive
# site, and rule `/ / 200` above already serves the hub for it. Offline, sw.js
# matches cached pages with ignoreSearch, so "/?m=1" still opens the cached hub.

/feeds/* / 301
/feed/posts/default / 301
/feed / 301
/atom.xml / 301
/rss.xml / 301
/search / 301
/search/* / 301


